c08ee3645692aa5630bc00d8dcd619fe4e6410b050afb6875339ab0962894ed2.png)\n\nConsider the diagram that is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $a =-c$\n- **II.** $c =-\\frac{1}{2}a$\n- **III.** $b = d$" 13194139534834 17592186045436 true] [17592186097176 1074 "The grid gives a=(6,4), c=(-3,-2), b=(-3,-5), d=(3,5). Thus c=-a/2; a=-c and b=d are false." 13194139534834 17592186045436 true] [17592186120421 1068 #edb/ref 17592186118640 13194139534834 17592186045436 false] [17592186120421 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402776 13194139534834 17592186045436 false] [17592186120421 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440235 13194139534834 17592186045436 true] [17592186120421 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440236 13194139534834 17592186045436 true] [17592186120421 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440237 13194139534834 17592186045436 true] [17592186120421 1071 "Which of the following matrices are upper triangular matrices?\n\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 7 & 11\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139534834 17592186045436 false] [17592186120421 1071 "Which of the following matrices are upper triangular matrices?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 7 & 11\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139534834 17592186045436 true] [17592186120426 1068 #edb/ref 17592186118670 13194139534834 17592186045436 false] [17592186120426 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440238 13194139534834 17592186045436 true] [17592186120426 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440239 13194139534834 17592186045436 true] [17592186120426 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440240 13194139534834 17592186045436 true] [17592186120426 1071 "Consider the matrix\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $A$ is upper triangular\n2. $A^{T} + A$ is upper triangular\n3. $A$ is lower triangular" 13194139534834 17592186045436 false] [17592186120426 1071 "Consider the matrix\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A$ is upper triangular\n- **II.** $A^{T} + A$ is upper triangular\n- **III.** $A$ is lower triangular" 13194139534834 17592186045436 true] [17592186120426 1074 "A is neither upper nor lower triangular because both off-diagonal entries a12=1 and a21=-1 are nonzero. However A^T+A=diag(-6,2,10), so statement II is true." 13194139534834 17592186045436 true] [17592186124945 1068 #edb/ref 17592186122270 13194139534834 17592186045436 false] [17592186124945 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440174 13194139534834 17592186045436 true] [17592186124945 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440175 13194139534834 17592186045436 true] [17592186124945 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440176 13194139534834 17592186045436 true] [17592186124945 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440177 13194139534834 17592186045436 true] [17592186124945 1071 "![](images/8e/8e62c3485aed6c52001cc7ad8eda5c1eb09cd4873d6b740eb7595413dafa15e0.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above correspond to an invertible function?" 13194139534834 17592186045436 false] [17592186124945 1071 "![](images/8e/8e62c3485aed6c52001cc7ad8eda5c1eb09cd4873d6b740eb7595413dafa15e0.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above correspond to an invertible function?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nGraph positions: **I.** upper left; **II.** upper right; **III.** lower left; **IV.** lower right." 13194139534834 17592186045436 true] [17592186124945 1074 "Graphs I and II are strictly monotone and pass the horizontal line test. Graphs III and IV each meet some horizontal line twice. There are four graphs, so all four labels are represented." 13194139534834 17592186045436 true] [17592186124970 1068 #edb/ref 17592186122420 13194139534834 17592186045436 false] [17592186124970 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440178 13194139534834 17592186045436 true] [17592186124970 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440179 13194139534834 17592186045436 true] [17592186124970 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440180 13194139534834 17592186045436 true] [17592186124970 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440181 13194139534834 17592186045436 true] [17592186124970 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440182 13194139534834 17592186045436 true] [17592186124970 1071 "At which points on the curve $2xy + 3y^{2} = x^{2} + 4$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $j$?" 13194139534834 17592186045436 false] [17592186124970 1071 "At which points on the curve $2xy + 3y^{2} = x^{2} + 4$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $j$?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked.\n\n- **I.** $(-1,-1)$\n- **II.** $(-1,0)$\n- **III.** $(0,1)$\n- **IV.** $(1,-1)$\n- **V.** $(1,1)$" 13194139534834 17592186045436 true] [17592186124970 1074 "Write the curve as F(x,y)=0. A tangent is (F_y,-F_x); its dot product with the given vector is $2 x - 2 y$. Solving this with the curve equation gives $(-1,-1), (1,1)$, and the tangent is nonzero at each point." 13194139534834 17592186045436 true] [17592186125018 1068 #edb/ref 17592186122708 13194139534834 17592186045436 false] [17592186125018 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440183 13194139534834 17592186045436 true] [17592186125018 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440184 13194139534834 17592186045436 true] [17592186125018 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440185 13194139534834 17592186045436 true] [17592186125018 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440186 13194139534834 17592186045436 true] [17592186125018 1071 "![](images/ca/caeed93f57e554d6bf79b8915935261abb39c6fe54cc6d7d349ff60502a96053.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above correspond to an invertible function?" 13194139534834 17592186045436 false] [17592186125018 1071 "![](images/ca/caeed93f57e554d6bf79b8915935261abb39c6fe54cc6d7d349ff60502a96053.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above correspond to an invertible function?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nGraph positions: **I.** upper left; **II.** upper right; **III.** lower left; **IV.** lower right." 13194139534834 17592186045436 true] [17592186125018 1074 "Graph I is strictly increasing. Graph II has a local minimum and maximum, graph III first decreases to a visible local minimum and then increases, and graph IV is a downward parabola. Thus II, III, and IV fail the horizontal line test; only I is invertible." 13194139534834 17592186045436 true] [17592186125023 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323313 13194139534834 17592186045436 false] [17592186125023 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440187 13194139534834 17592186045436 true] [17592186125023 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440188 13194139534834 17592186045436 true] [17592186125023 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440189 13194139534834 17592186045436 true] [17592186125023 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440190 13194139534834 17592186045436 true] [17592186125023 1071 "Given $h(x,y,z)=xy+x{y}^{2}+{z}^{3}.$ Find the points $(x,y,z)$ where $∇h(x,y,z)=0.$" 13194139534834 17592186045436 false] [17592186125023 1071 "Given $h(x,y,z)=xy+x{y}^{2}+{z}^{3}.$ Find the points $(x,y,z)$ where $∇h(x,y,z)=0.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked.\n\n- **I.** $(-1,-1,-1)$\n- **II.** $(0,-1,0)$\n- **III.** $(0,0,0)$\n- **IV.** $(1,-1,1)$" 13194139534834 17592186045436 true] [17592186125023 1074 "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy+x{y}^{2}+{z}^{3}) \\\\  & =y+{y}^{2}+0 \\\\  & =y+{y}^{2} \\\\ {h}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy+x{y}^{2}+{z}^{3}) \\\\  & =x+2xy+0 \\\\  & =x+2xy \\\\ {h}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy+x{y}^{2}+{z}^{3}) \\\\  & =0+0+3{z}^{2} \\\\  & =3{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇h(x,y,z)=(y+{y}^{2})\\,i+(x+2xy)\\,j+3{z}^{2}\\,k.$\n\nNow, we solve $∇f(x,y,z)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+{y}^{2})\\,i+(x+2xy)\\,j+3{z}^{2}\\,k & =0\\text{ }i+0\\text{ }j+0\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $i$, $j$, and $k$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y+{y}^{2}=0 \\\\ x+2xy=0 \\\\ 3{z}^{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first equation can be written as $y(1+y)=0$, which gives $y=0$ or $y=-1.$ Substituting these into the second equation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2x(0) & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2x(-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, from the third equation, we get $z=0.$\n\nTherefore, $∇h(x,y,z)=0$ at two points: $(0,0,0)$ and $(0,-1,0).$" 13194139534834 17592186045436 false] [17592186125023 1074 "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy+x{y}^{2}+{z}^{3}) \\\\  & =y+{y}^{2}+0 \\\\  & =y+{y}^{2} \\\\ {h}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy+x{y}^{2}+{z}^{3}) \\\\  & =x+2xy+0 \\\\  & =x+2xy \\\\ {h}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy+x{y}^{2}+{z}^{3}) \\\\  & =0+0+3{z}^{2} \\\\  & =3{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇h(x,y,z)=(y+{y}^{2})\\,i+(x+2xy)\\,j+3{z}^{2}\\,k.$\n\nNow, we solve $∇h(x,y,z)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+{y}^{2})\\,i+(x+2xy)\\,j+3{z}^{2}\\,k & =0\\text{ }i+0\\text{ }j+0\\text{ }k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $i$, $j$, and $k$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{cases}y+{y}^{2}=0 \\\\ x+2xy=0 \\\\ 3{z}^{2}=0\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nThe first equation can be written as $y(1+y)=0$, which gives $y=0$ or $y=-1.$ Substituting these into the second equation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2x(0) & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2x(-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, from the third equation, we get $z=0.$\n\nTherefore, $∇h(x,y,z)=0$ at two points: $(0,0,0)$ and $(0,-1,0).$" 13194139534834 17592186045436 true] [17592186125192 1068 #edb/ref 17592186123752 13194139534834 17592186045436 false] [17592186125192 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440286 13194139534834 17592186045436 true] [