equating the coefficient of $i$, $j$, and $k$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{z+y}{{x}^{2}yz}=0 \\\\ -\\frac{1+x}{x{y}^{2}}=0 \\\\ -\\frac{1+{z}^{2}x}{{z}^{2}x}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x=-1.$ Substituting these into the third equation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1+{z}^{2}x}{{z}^{2}x} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{1+{z}^{2}(-1)}{{z}^{2}(-1)} & =0 \\\\ 1-{z}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ z & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-1$ into the first equation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{z+y}{(-1)^{2}yz} & =0 \\\\ z+y & =0 \\\\ y & =-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $z=1,$ we have $y=-1$ and when $z=-1,$ we have $y=1.$\n\nHence, $∇f(x,y,z)=0$ at two points: $(-1,-1,1)$ and $(-1,1,-1).$" 13194139534834 17592186045436 false] [17592186403475 1074 "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{xy}+\\frac{1}{y}+\\frac{1}{zx}-z) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}+0-\\frac{1}{z{x}^{2}}-0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{z+y}{{x}^{2}yz} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{xy}+\\frac{1}{y}+\\frac{1}{zx}-z) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x{y}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}+0-0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1+x}{x{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{1}{xy}+\\frac{1}{y}+\\frac{1}{zx}-z) \\\\  & =0+0-\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}x}-1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1+{z}^{2}x}{{z}^{2}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(x,y,z)=-\\frac{z+y}{{x}^{2}yz}\\,i-\\frac{1+x}{x{y}^{2}}\\,j-\\frac{1+{z}^{2}x}{{z}^{2}x}\\,k.$ \n\nNow, we solve $∇f(x,y,z)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{z+y}{{x}^{2}yz}\\,i-\\frac{1+x}{x{y}^{2}}\\,j-\\frac{1+{z}^{2}x}{{z}^{2}x}\\,k & =0\\text{ }i+0\\text{ }j+0\\text{ }k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficient of $i$, $j$, and $k$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{cases}-\\frac{z+y}{{x}^{2}yz}=0 \\\\ -\\frac{1+x}{x{y}^{2}}=0 \\\\ -\\frac{1+{z}^{2}x}{{z}^{2}x}=0\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x=-1.$ Substituting these into the third equation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1+{z}^{2}x}{{z}^{2}x} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{1+{z}^{2}(-1)}{{z}^{2}(-1)} & =0 \\\\ 1-{z}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ z & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-1$ into the first equation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{z+y}{(-1)^{2}yz} & =0 \\\\ z+y & =0 \\\\ y & =-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $z=1,$ we have $y=-1$ and when $z=-1,$ we have $y=1.$\n\nHence, $∇f(x,y,z)=0$ at two points: $(-1,-1,1)$ and $(-1,1,-1).$" 13194139534834 17592186045436 true] [17592186410576 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410574 13194139534834 17592186045436 false] [17592186410576 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440357 13194139534834 17592186045436 true] [17592186410576 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440358 13194139534834 17592186045436 true] [17592186410576 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440359 13194139534834 17592186045436 true] [17592186410576 1071 "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $F=({e}^{x+y}-\\sqrt{2})\\,i+({e}^{x+y}-\\sqrt{7})\\,j$\n- $G=({y}^{3}-6x)\\,i+(3x{y}^{2})\\,j$\n- $H=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\,j$" 13194139534834 17592186045436 false] [17592186410576 1071 "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $F=({e}^{x+y}-\\sqrt{2})\\,i+({e}^{x+y}-\\sqrt{7})\\,j$\n- **II.** $G=({y}^{3}-6x)\\,i+(3x{y}^{2})\\,j$\n- **III.** $H=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\,j$" 13194139534834 17592186045436 true] [17592186437021 1068 #edb/ref 17592186437013 13194139534834 17592186045436 false] [17592186437021 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440308 13194139534834 17592186045436 true] [17592186437021 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440309 13194139534834 17592186045436 true] [17592186437021 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440310 13194139534834 17592186045436 true] [17592186437021 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440311 13194139534834 17592186045436 true] [17592186437021 1071 "Given $h(x,y,z)=xy{e}^{x}+{z}^{2}{e}^{z}.$ Find the points $(x,y,z)$ where $∇h(x,y,z)=0.$" 13194139534834 17592186045436 false] [17592186437021 1071 "Given $h(x,y,z)=xy{e}^{x}+{z}^{2}{e}^{z}.$ Find the points $(x,y,z)$ where $∇h(x,y,z)=0.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked.\n\n- **I.** $(-1,0,-2)$\n- **II.** $(0,0,-2)$\n- **III.** $(0,0,0)$\n- **IV.** $(0,0,2)$" 13194139534834 17592186045436 true] [17592186437021 1074 "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy{e}^{x}+{z}^{2}{e}^{z}) \\\\  & =xy{e}^{x}+y{e}^{x}+0 \\\\  & =y(x+1){e}^{x} \\\\ {h}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy{e}^{x}+{z}^{2}{e}^{z}) \\\\  & =x{e}^{x}+0 \\\\  & =x{e}^{x} \\\\ {h}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy{e}^{x}+{z}^{2}{e}^{z}) \\\\  & =0+2z{e}^{z}+{z}^{2}{e}^{z} \\\\  & =z(2+z){e}^{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇h(x,y,z)=y(x+1){e}^{x}\\,i+x{e}^{x}\\,j+z(2+z){e}^{z}\\,k.$\n\nNow, we solve $∇f(x,y,z)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x+1){e}^{x}\\,i+x{e}^{x}\\,j+z(2+z){e}^{z}\\,k & =0\\text{ }i+0\\text{ }j+0\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $i$, $j$, and $k$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y(x+1){e}^{x}=0 \\\\ x{e}^{x}=0 \\\\ z(2+z){e}^{z}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe second equation gives $x=0.$ Substituting these into the first equation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0+1){e}^{0} & =0 \\\\ y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, from the third equation, we get  $z=0$ or $z=-2.$\n\nTherefore, $∇h(x,y,z)=0$ at two points: $(0,0,0)$ and $(0,0,-2).$" 13194139534834 17592186045436 false] [17592186437021 1074 "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy{e}^{x}+{z}^{2}{e}^{z}) \\\\  & =xy{e}^{x}+y{e}^{x}+0 \\\\  & =y(x+1){e}^{x} \\\\ {h}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy{e}^{x}+{z}^{2}{e}^{z}) \\\\  & =x{e}^{x}+0 \\\\  & =x{e}^{x} \\\\ {h}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy{e}^{x}+{z}^{2}{e}^{z}) \\\\  & =0+2z{e}^{z}+{z}^{2}{e}^{z} \\\\  & =z(2+z){e}^{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇h(x,y,z)=y(x+1){e}^{x}\\,i+x{e}^{x}\\,j+z(2+z){e}^{z}\\,k.$\n